  "comment": "Cross-implementation anchor for the convergence rules (kernel/src/convergence.ts): given what a round states about itself, what the loop does next (5.7 R40 scenario 4). This is policy arithmetic over verdicts somebody else produced — nothing here verifies anything. Rules a second implementation must reproduce, stated here so it need not read the TypeScript: (1) the input object is `iterate.convergence/0.1` (the version key is optional) with required `round` (integer >=1), `maxRounds` (integer >=1), `invariantState` in `pass|fail|error|unmeasured`, `openFindings` (integer >=0), `evidenceComplete` (boolean), `breakerLevel` (integer 0..3, where 3 is tripped); no other keys. (2) PRECEDENCE, first match wins: breakerLevel >= 3 -> halt, reason `circuit-breaker-tripped`; round >= maxRounds -> halt, reason `round-budget-exhausted`; invariantState `error` -> halt, reason `invariant-check-errored`; openFindings > 0 -> fix, reason `open-findings`; invariantState `fail` -> fix, reason `invariants-failed`; evidenceComplete false -> verify, reason `evidence-incomplete`; invariantState `unmeasured` -> verify, reason `invariants-unmeasured`; otherwise -> converged, reason `all-gates-green`. (3) WHY that order: a limit the harness set on itself is not the app's failure, so the breaker halts before anything is counted; the budget is checked before the work so a run cannot buy another round by re-reporting findings it already had; a check that errored yields no verdict, and retrying it is the runner's job, not the loop's; `verify` rather than `converged` for unlooked-or-unmeasured states is the whole point — 'we could not look' and 'we looked and it holds' must not collapse into the same line. (4) The decision echoes `round` and `maxRounds` unchanged, so a ledger line says which round was judged. (5) `breakerLevel` is the engine's own circuit-breaker level for the run; 3 is the policy limit, which is why the range stops there. PROVENANCE: max_rounds 7 is the repo's own `config/iterate.config.yaml`; the breaker ceiling of 3 is `CircuitBreakerLevelSchema` in the evidence-pack contract; each case below states its inputs and the reason the precedence gives, and the expected values were derived from the rule text in this file — not by running the TypeScript.  AMENDMENT (0.1.8): `breakerLevel` is OPTIONAL. Absent means this run has no circuit-breaker channel at all and the first precedence rule is skipped; it is NOT the same claim as 0, which asserts a breaker exists and reports it untripped. A producer that never looked must omit the key rather than write 0.",
